Multiply both sides by $(x + 1)$ and collect the $x$ terms:
\begin{align*}
y(x + 1) &= 3x - 2 \\
xy + y &= 3x - 2 \\
xy - 3x &= -2 - y \\
x(y - 3) &= -2 - y \\
x &= \frac{-2 - y}{y - 3} = \frac{y + 2}{3 - y}
\end{align*}
This is valid for $y \neq 3$; the line $y = 3$ is the horizontal asymptote of the original function.

Taking $u = 0$, $a = 9.8\,\text{m s}^{-2}$ and $t = 2.0\,\text{s}$:
\begin{align*}
v &= u + at \\
&= 0 + 9.8 \times 2.0 \\
&= 19.6\,\text{m s}^{-1}
\end{align*}
